2186534681}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534681} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3d9a905-bd06-5c37-a014-3005c06bd023" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f860a2b3-6e2b-5a0c-99c8-165c277a9c9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-331796" :question/problem "Consider a function $z(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{z(t)\\}=Z(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{6t}z(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{6t}z(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{6t}z(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{6t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-6)t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $Z(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-6)t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t=Z(s-6),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-6>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+6.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d715b5d0-cb33-5478-a9fe-0e3da862463c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535122}}] :activity/title "The First Shifting Theorem of Laplace Transforms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534264}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549098} :activity/id #uuid "aee98487-9e85-584a-97ef-6d3596a81ff9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050615 :topic/id #uuid "58ea9fa1-e1b7-56f7-a58d-173ed320b2af" :topic/math-academy-id 6369} :ac