er/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{(s+9)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368074} {:answer/id #uuid "670a204b-dbd0-5762-9954-fc001e5f533d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{(s-9)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368075}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368072} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec7afe59-29fd-5b24-8255-f5b581a6ba68" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368071} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a989fc-e8e2-5596-b19b-18c8318b979c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{st}\\cdot {e}^{-9t}q(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368077} {:answer/id #uuid "e712aa1e-27e0-5c55-b9b9-c4995f8ad8e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-9t}q(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368078} {:answer/id #uuid "0b9f5ac0-0ad4-510b-8d06-44e1d96e1da7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{st}\\cdot {e}^{9t}q(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368079} {:answer/id #uuid "2bda30ae-8042-58bd-8658-26b8557e0ed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{9t}q(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368080}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368080} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ff60236-5483-532f-997e-f3321f9ebbbf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f9cd6f01-d8c7-5536-9f5a-869210d50728" :question/math-academy-id "q-331799" :question/problem "Consider a function $q(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{q(t)\\}=Q(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{9t}q(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{9t}q(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{9t}q(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{9t}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-9)t}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $Q(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-9)t}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t=Q(s-9),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-9>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+9.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "470da428-0379-5a0d-992c-de8783330311" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549150}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549098 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50798ffc-3732-5591-ba0b-04ece45c7da1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{(s-3)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548505} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5f41d3-f5c2-5c17-99da-23a331941594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{6}{(s-3)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548525} {:answer/id #uuid "83c7c20a-5a9a-5e9c-92d4-1db64ddad7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(s-3)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548607} {:answer/id #uuid "9a625c27-70a6-5cf6-bcf3-a7be0cbcc47f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{(s-3)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548661} {:answer/id #uuid "9fb78983-301f-5651-9fb7-1ab6e18840f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{6}{(s-3)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548667}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548667} :answer-field/id #uuid "657c492a-d1c0-573d-a492-e9c1d9f752bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "998b7976-7802-594e-8c46-4a5b40b28b4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-64298" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=5t+3{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}$ for $s>3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{5t+3{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{5t\\}+L\\{3{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =5L\\{t\\}+3L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{t\\} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ L\\{{t}^{2}\\} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\}=\\frac{2}{(s-3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =5L\\{t\\}+3L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{2}{(s-3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{6}{(s-3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>3.$"} :step/id #uuid "517ae331-e78e-5e2c-9909-0a4fabd9ed67" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549104}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549104 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390920 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aafd302f-6a35-5d36-98f2-cf4624e295be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{st}\\cdot {e}^{4t}y(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390922} {:answer/id #uuid "66bf87d7-e6c2-5282-850d-eb9aebd5c3b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-st}\\cdo