 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bf25d1c4-52d6-5c8a-89b9-2f0b3253eb28" :question/math-academy-id "q-331792" :question/problem "Consider a function $w(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{w(t)\\}=W(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{-6t}w(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-6t}w(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,w(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-6t}w(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-6t}\\,w(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+6)t}\\,w(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $W(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,w(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+6)t}\\,w(t)\\,\\text{d}t=W(s+6),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+6>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-6.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4352ef4f-23b6-5c61-ac25-56e4098a43c6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551385}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551466 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f4facf-480c-575f-b09c-5c3bfbccd39a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-(s-3)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550382} {:answer/id #uuid "21fff9cb-25a4-576b-bbc7-e8761dd6a9ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-(s+3)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550443} {:answer/id #uuid "8d0f5080-456e-5190-ba35-fb2f45ed0d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{(s-3)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550728} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd3ad9c-eb6c-5075-bd57-91281dbc1bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{(s+3)t}" :db